{"id":"h165","run":2,"role":"calibration","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Assume that all gases are perfect and that data refer to 298.15 K unless otherwise stated. A sample consisting of $3.00 \\mathrm{~mol}$ of diatomic perfect gas molecules at $200 \\mathrm{~K}$ is compressed reversibly and adiabatically until its temperature reaches $250 \\mathrm{~K}$. Given that $C_{V, \\mathrm{~m}}=27.5 \\mathrm{~J} \\mathrm{~K}^{-1} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$, calculate $\\Delta U$.\nIs the correct answer \"+7.0 kJ\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The correct value of ΔU is about +4.1 kJ, not +7.0 kJ.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"For a perfect gas, ΔU depends only on the temperature change, ΔU = n·C_V,m·ΔT.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The process is adiabatic, so q = 0 and the work done on the gas equals ΔU.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"The temperature change is ΔT = 50 K.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Calculation gives ΔU = 3.00 mol × 27.5 J/K/mol × 50 K = 4125 J ≈ +4.1 kJ.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"None of the problem data directly supports +7.0 kJ.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-1,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The 7.0 kJ figure might come from a wrong heat capacity or temperature change, but no standard reading gives it.","confidence":0.3,"lean":-0.5,"theta":0.4712,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The direct calculation is the deciding factor, and +7.0 kJ does not match it.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":1,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.9,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.7,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.7,"relation":"suppor